  "text": "Solution 3 gives the correct final answer, 2, and I believe it matches the source result.\n\nI'm working from memory of Lin and Xu's paper on traveling waves of 2D inhomogeneous Euler equations in a finite channel, which I recall stating velocity in H^{3/2−} and density in H^{1/2−} as the flexible regime, with rigidity in H^{3/2+} × H^{1/2+} when the distorted Rayleigh operator has no eigenvalues or embedded eigenvalues. I couldn't check the paper here.\n\nThe other two fail on their own reasoning:\n- **Solution 1** takes s_v = 1/2 from the streamfunction's logarithmic singularity. That conflates the regularity of the streamfunction with that of the velocity, which is one derivative higher, so the velocity exponent is 3/2.\n- **Solution 2** takes s_ρ = −1/2 from a 1/(U−c) singularity. The density perturbation is not that singular at the critical layer, so the exponent is not −1/2.\n\nSolution 3's density step is also shaky: it writes the density perturbation with a log singularity and gets s_ρ = 1/2, which agrees with the paper's density exponent. Its velocity step (s_v = 3/2) also agrees. Its final sum, 3/2 + 1/2 = 2, is the answer I expect.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
